WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.280
The $1298 bought the 1977 Apple II, even though its chips came from catalogs. This is

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:12.760
First Principles Daily, episode 137. After those design choices, a First Principle Spotlight

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.500
on the material floor, then the hobby kit market they replaced, and what the remaining

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.620
gaps still left open. Steve Wozniak built the machine from parts a distributor would sell

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:29.920
to anyone. A MOS technology 6502, dynamic RAM, and ordinary discrete parts. He did not

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.580
stop at a bare processor board. Color video went on to the motherboard, so a standard television

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.940
became the display without a separate box. 8 expansion slots let an owner add memory

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.180
or peripherals later instead of buying a larger machine on day one. Rod Holt replaced

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.120
the heavy linear supply common in other systems with a switching supply that cut weight and heat.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:57.400
Jari Manok gave it a foam-molded plastic case, a finished shell rather than an open chassis.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:03.660
Those choices were made one at a time in 1976, while Wozniak was still at Hewlett Packard.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:09.040
Each one was checked against a single goal, unpack the machine, and use it with almost nothing

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:09.660
else to buy.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.100
A switching supply runs at higher frequency, so the transformers and capacitors can be smaller

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.720
than the ones in a linear supply.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.440
That is a real cut in copper and iron.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.640
The finished retail price still had to carry the cost of designing that supply and qualifying

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:25.980
it for safety in a home.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.700
The board only listing was $598, and that bought 4 kilobytes of RAM.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.740
Case, keyboard, and power supply brought the complete system to $1298.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.180
Split that gap, and the leftover money is not hiding in the processor.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.880
It sits in the enclosure, the assembly, the documentation, and integer basic already

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:47.780
burned into ROM.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:54.180
Now, the magic wand, wave it, arrange the silicon, the discrete parts, and the bear board

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.980
for free except for the atoms and the raw stock, and a rough estimate of that floor sits

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.080
well below even the board only price.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:08.560
Exact commodity prices for 1977 DRAM and the 6502 are not in public price lists, so

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.800
the floor stays a rough estimate, not a receipt.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.500
You can still see the shape of the idiot index.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.400
Take the finished sticker, $1298, and set it against the board only $598.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.440
$1298 divided by $598 lands a little above 2.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.700
Call that roughly 2-1 for the lowest configuration, and remember what you just divided.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:36.060
The denominator is already a populated board with markup, not a pile of raw silicon.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:42.800
Push the denominator down to that lower magic wand floor, and the index on the finished system climbs further.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.060
A high-idia index is a signal.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.140
The screaming cost is integration and presentation, not the chips.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:55.000
Wozniak later wrote in bite that a personal computer should be small, reliable, convenient,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.000
and inexpensive.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.480
The redesign went after convenience and reliability by moving functions onto the motherboard.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:06.300
The slots were a refusal to lock the buyer into one configuration at the factory.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.840
Memory and interfaces stayed unsoddered until the owner chose them, so the base bill of

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.500
materials stayed lower, while the case and the supply still had to support whatever came

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:16.860
later.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.180
Before that machine, most microcomputers left the store as bare boards or partial kits.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:28.320
The buyer supplied the enclosure, a linear power supply, an RF modulator, and often the

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:28.700
monitor.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:33.680
At clubs such as the Homebrew Computer Club, people built from published schematics

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.280
and whatever chips they could source.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.180
The culture treated the computer as an ongoing project, not a finished product.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:45.120
Kit prices often started several hundred dollars lower because the buyer accepted the

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.220
soldering and the chance that the thing would not fully work.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.700
Steve Jobs had already watched that pattern on the Apple one.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.080
He wanted the next machine aimed at households and schools.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.240
The old assumption was that a serious user would tolerate exposed boards and custom wiring.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.660
That kept the paper part count down.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.500
It also kept the volume down because only people willing to finish the assembly would buy.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:12.580
The default path in that market was a small improvement on the last hobbyist design.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.040
Wozniak declined that limit when he laid out the new board.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.880
Most earlier hobby machines treated video as an add-on.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.260
You bought an RF modulator or a dedicated monitor, and you carried that cost yourself.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:30.180
Moving the video circuitry onto the main board made the television a standard display

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.320
and took away a layer of hardware every buyer had previously sourced alone.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.700
The machine produced color graphics from its own circuitry.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.820
Leaving those decisions to the owner had lowered the sticker, it had also narrowed who

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:44.900
would walk out with one.

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:49.869
Once Vizical appeared, the Apple II reached schools and small businesses.

00:04:50.509 --> 00:04:53.729
Its margins stayed wider than those of later commodity machines.

00:04:54.409 --> 00:05:00.349
Volume in 1977 was still modest, so the learning that later high volume runs would bring had

00:05:00.349 --> 00:05:01.529
not shown up in the price.

00:05:01.529 --> 00:05:05.789
case. Further compression would have needed either those higher volumes or a different

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:11.289
way of making cases and power supplies. Neither happened at Apple that year. The molded

00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:16.169
case and the eight slots preserved expandability and a finished look at the expense of the lowest

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:22.329
possible price. Later clones would simplify or strip both. Full molding added material

00:05:22.329 --> 00:05:27.129
and tooling that a simple sheet metal box would have skipped. And it also spared the buyer

00:05:27.129 --> 00:05:28.489
from fabricating a housing.

00:05:29.189 --> 00:05:33.789
The supply, more efficient than a linear one, still needed its own certification and

00:05:33.789 --> 00:05:34.249
sourcing.

00:05:34.809 --> 00:05:37.389
Work a kit builder would have done one unit at a time.

00:05:37.989 --> 00:05:42.409
Those pieces kept the gap between board cost and system price from closing in the first

00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:42.709
year.

00:05:43.369 --> 00:05:47.449
The machine worked as a product because the purchaser did not have to solve the integration.

00:05:48.029 --> 00:05:51.569
The same decisions left later competitors a clear place to attack.

00:05:52.189 --> 00:05:53.569
The enclosure and the supply.

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:58.996
When the visible cost of a machine is the case and the integration, not the processor,

00:05:59.536 --> 00:06:01.516
the constraint you are looking at is assembly.

00:06:02.156 --> 00:06:04.116
Silicon was already a catalog item.

00:06:04.676 --> 00:06:07.736
The leap was deciding which jobs still deserve their own box.

00:06:08.476 --> 00:06:12.996
Treating the television as the display deleted a whole category of dedicated hardware that

00:06:12.996 --> 00:06:15.876
hobbyist designs had kept carrying forward out of habit.

00:06:16.536 --> 00:06:19.096
That is reasoning by analogy in physical form.

00:06:19.656 --> 00:06:23.056
Last year's interface copied because last year's builder had one.

00:06:23.056 --> 00:06:28.316
1. First Principles asks a blunter question. Does this function need its own part, its

00:06:28.316 --> 00:06:32.996
own supply, its own certification, or can it live on the board the buyer already paid

00:06:32.996 --> 00:06:33.256
for?

00:06:33.756 --> 00:06:39.016
The Apple II answered that for video, and only partly for power and housing. Holds supply

00:06:39.016 --> 00:06:43.756
got closer to the material floor by shrinking magnetics, then stepped away from that floor

00:06:43.756 --> 00:06:46.216
again when safety qualification entered the price.

00:06:46.916 --> 00:06:51.776
Manox case did the same with tooling. A low idiot index would have meant the finished thing

00:06:51.776 --> 00:06:53.216
was near the cost of its atoms.

00:06:53.836 --> 00:06:58.816
A high one, which this system still had, meant the factory steps between catalog chips and

00:06:58.816 --> 00:07:00.536
a schoolroom appliance were the prize.

00:07:01.216 --> 00:07:04.736
You see the same shift whenever a platform moves from kit to appliance.

00:07:05.356 --> 00:07:09.596
Someone draws a line between what the user will assemble and what must arrive working.

00:07:10.176 --> 00:07:14.736
Everything on the wrong side of that line becomes enclosure, qualification, and software

00:07:14.736 --> 00:07:18.256
in ROM, and the sticker jumps even if the processor does not.

00:07:18.756 --> 00:07:20.996
The useful habit is to write the list twice.

00:07:20.996 --> 00:07:26.736
Once as raw parts anyone can buy, once as the finished object, a non-builder will accept.

00:07:27.336 --> 00:07:31.056
The distance between those lists is the idiot index you can actually attack.

00:07:31.636 --> 00:07:35.656
What would change if the next generation of educational hardware started from the same

00:07:35.656 --> 00:07:38.476
list of catalog parts instead of from last year's case?

